{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And that you may tell, etc.</b> One must know for what purpose this second reason was necessary, and why the first reason — that HaShem desired to place these signs — as I explained just above, did not suffice. Further, what is meant by ״אֵת אֲשֶׁר הִתְעַלַּלְתִּי בְּמִצְרַיִם,״ and what is meant by ״וְאֶת אֹתֹתַי,״ which appear to be two separate things. Further, one must understand the intent of His saying <b>and you shall know</b>, etc.:" "<b>Indeed</b> the intent of the verse is that HaShem came to make known that the ultimate purpose of bringing the signs into his midst was not to take vengeance upon Pharaoh, but rather to reinforce the signs through which the foundation of faith is rooted in the heart of Israel, so that it be engraved never to be forgotten forever — for when it is in the midst of Pharaoh it will be in the memory of the children of Israel for all eternity. And the intent follows what they said, of blessed memory, in tractate Ḥullin (Ḥullin 75a) regarding the law of a ben peku'ah: whenever there are two wondrous things involved, it will be constantly remembered, and when they see it being eaten without slaughter they will not suspect it, because it is remembered among them, for ״תְּרֵי תְּמִיהֵי מִדְכַּר דְּכִירֵי אִינְשֵׁי״ — two wonders make a thing remembered by people. Now, through the wonder alone that HaShem would perform to make known that He rules over wind, water, dust, living creatures, fire, air, and the like, this would not suffice for the matter to be remembered in perpetual permanence so as to engrave the faith; rather, it requires the existence of a second wonder — that HaShem would perform wonders to bring evil upon Pharaoh and his servants, to make them drink cups of wormwood, such as was not done to any nation. And this is for you the pleasantness of delightful sayings: ״וּלְמַעַן תְּסַפֵּר בְּאָזְנֵי בִנְךָ״ — this matter, and the telling of what would otherwise be impossible to keep constantly in your memory except through the agency of two wonders. The first is <b>אֵת אֲשֶׁר הִתְעַלַּלְתִּי בְּמִצְרַיִם</b> — behold, one wonder: even though HaShem was bringing upon them afflictions that did not involve disruption of the natural order and its laws, nevertheless, given the multitude of troubles and plagues — ten plagues, and each plague comprised four or five plagues — ״לֹא עָשָׂה כֵן לְכָל גּוֹי״; behold, the thing is astonishing. Yet this wonder alone is not ״דְּכִירֵי אִינְשֵׁי״ and they would not tell of the deeds of HaShem. And He said <b>וְאֶת אֹתֹתַי</b> — meaning, the signs that HaShem performed are a second wonder, for even apart from the Egyptian matter, the sign in and of itself is a wondrous thing. And through the agency of two wonders ״תְּסַפֵּר בְּאָזְנֵי בִנְךָ וּבֶן בִנְךָ״, etc. And the ultimate goal intended in the telling is ״וִידַעְתֶּם כִּי אֲנִי ה׳״ — and there is no other, and you shall renounce all faiths besides this. And so you will find that when HaShem blessed be He spoke with us at Mount Sinai, He prefaced His words, blessed be He, saying ״אָנֹכִי ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ,״ and this for you is the sign: ״אֲשֶׁר הוֹצֵאתִיךָ מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם.״ And it is this that He prefaced His words here and said ״וִידַעְתֶּם כִּי אֲנִי ה׳,״ for through this they would recognize by sensory perception the truth of the faith, something no human being from one end of the world to the other had ever attained. Blessed is He who did thus for us."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:26:29.263597823Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}